  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 AM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nCurrently, mid/upper troughing is centered off to the northeast, \nalong with surface high pressure. A frontal boundary that produced a \nlot of the recent rainfall is along and south of I-40. This has \nkept drier and milder air in the north with deep moisture \nremaining in southern portions of the area. As such, additional \nshowers and isolate storms are likely today, focused towards \nsoutheast Tennessee, southwest North Carolina, and the East \nTennessee mountains. Based on the CAMs, better coverage and \nintensity remains south, but recent saturation will pose at least \nan isolated flooding threat in those areas.\n\nTonight into Saturday, this boundary will weaken and track further \nnorth with another shortwave swinging through. Overall, moisture \nwill be lessened with minimal instability, likely resulting in \nmainly showers. By Sunday, the shortwave will deepen to our east \nwith ridging and high pressure expanding from the west. With drier \nair in place, rain chances will be limited, but height rises will \npush temperatures further into the 80s to near 90.\n\nThe synoptic pattern will remain fairly unchanged throughout much of \nnext week with a 5,940m to 5,970m high centered over the desert \nsouthwest and a general troughing pattern to our east. There are \nvarying indications of shortwaves moving through the region, \npotentially moreso around Wednesday. The general northwest flow \npattern will keep chances for showers and storms in the forecast \nwith eventual shortwaves elevating the coverage. The more limited \ncoverage should certainly decrease the flooding threat well below \nwhat has been seen recently.",
